1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,391
One releaseGate call. Three aspects.
Security must reject unauthorized
operators before anything else runs.
Transactions must begin before business
work and commit after.

2
00:00:13,511 --> 00:00:20,591
Metrics want to time the call — ideally
without opening a database transaction for
callers who were going to be denied
anyway.

3
00:00:20,711 --> 00:00:31,968
If the order is accidental, you open
connections for rejected trucks, or you
time only the inner fragment and lie on
the dashboard. Aspect ordering makes that
sequence intentional.

4
00:00:32,088 --> 00:00:46,415
Spring uses Ordered / @Order on aspect
beans (and on some infrastructure
advisors). Lower order values mean higher
precedence. For @Around advice, higher
precedence is outer: enters first, exits
last.

5
00:00:46,536 --> 00:01:02,088
Think nested wrappers. For harbor gate
releases, prefer security → transaction →
metrics from outside in. Look at this java
for a moment. Look at this java for a
moment. Look at this java for a moment.

6
00:01:02,207 --> 00:01:16,152
Narrate the enter path. Client hits the
proxy. Security (order 1) checks
permission, calls proceed. Transactional-
style advice (order 2) begins work, calls
proceed.

7
00:01:16,271 --> 00:01:26,471
Metrics (order 3) starts a timer, calls
proceed, hits the target. On the way out:
metrics stop, transaction commits,
security exits.

8
00:01:26,591 --> 00:01:38,543
Unauthorized callers fail in security and
never open a transaction — usually what
you want. Swap carelessly and you hold
database connections for operators who
were never allowed to release the gate.

9
00:01:38,663 --> 00:01:49,895
Spring’s own @Transactional and @Async
advisors live in the same precedence
world. Custom aspects that must run
outside or inside transactions should
declare @Order explicitly.

10
00:01:50,015 --> 00:01:57,599
When two aspects share the same order
value, relative order is effectively
undefined — do not depend on class-name
sorting luck.

11
00:01:57,719 --> 00:02:10,655
Within one aspect class, advice-type
precedence has its own rules; cross-aspect
problems are what @Order on the aspect
bean solves. Prefer one clear
responsibility per aspect so ordering
stays reviewable.

12
00:02:10,775 --> 00:02:21,983
Misconception: “@Order on any @Component
controls method call order in the app.”
Here it controls advisor precedence in the
AOP chain, not general bean
initialization.

13
00:02:22,103 --> 00:02:37,007
Another: expecting @After from a lower-
precedence aspect to run before @After
from a higher-precedence aspect without
drawing the enter/exit nest. Draw the
nest. Speak the nest. Then the numbers
make sense.

14
00:02:37,127 --> 00:02:50,495
Ordered aspects keep security,
transactions, and metrics from tripping
over each other. They do not erase the
cost of every proxy hop and advice
invocation. When does that cost matter on
a busy gate?

15
00:02:50,675 --> 00:02:53,411
That is AOP performance.
